.SH DESCRIPTION
ssmcp provides a local shell, managed command jobs and file tools.
Run ssmcp username@server to connect over HTTPS and WSS. The first connection
opens GitHub authorization, binds the chosen server-local username after browser
confirmation, and continues connecting. Later connections reuse the credential
for that server. Bare ssmcp reuses the last authorized account.
No separate login command, SSH key or repeated \-\-allow is required.
.PP
The \-\-ssh switch or explicit SSH options select legacy OpenSSH public-key authentication. An existing
client login can supply the user's verified GitHub identity for the SSH allow
policy; explicit \-\-allow remains available for sharing with other users.
Without a client login or \-\-allow, SSH connections return the account's
/username URL and a one-time login code. GitHub-authorized connections return
/mcp. Each connection has a random client ID.
.PP
ssmcp discovers installed Codex, Claude Code, Pi and OpenCode executables on PATH;
optional harness failures leave other capabilities available. Multiple clients
may connect under the same account.
.PP
For foreground clients, interactive standard output shows a connection summary. Redirected output or
JSONL mode emits one JSON object containing url, client_id, name, type, hostname
and cwd. The type is shell. GitHub mode adds auth:"github" and the canonical
allow list; login-code mode adds login_code and login_expires_in. Live activity and
diagnostics use standard error. The cwd field is the startup directory; it does
not track shell directory changes or the workspaces of native agent sessions.
.PP
The external MCP catalog contains exactly two tools: list and code_mode.
Use list to discover clients and their native capabilities. Use code_mode to
run JavaScript that calls a selected capability through its live connection.
One registry owns all Shell, workspace, Harness and MCP routes. Only usable native interfaces are
registered; installation alone is not a tool inventory. Native names, schemas,
results, errors and approval requests retain their meaning through namespaced
routes. Each call checks the live connection, provider and current authorization.
.PP
Keep the program running while its tools are in use. Ending the connection
stops its owned background jobs and closes native provider connections.
Operator-managed external Pi and OpenCode services are not terminated. Login-code grants issued by that
connection are revoked. GitHub identity grants remain subject to their own
lifetimes and each currently connected client's allow policy.
.SH CLIENT ACCOUNTS AND TEMPORARY ACCESS
.TP
.B ssmcp username@server
Validate the username, automatically obtain GitHub authorization when needed,
and connect this device. Use \-\-no-browser to print the authorization link and
confirmation code for another device. The authorized server becomes the default.
Names are case-insensitive and stored in lowercase. Use 3 through 32 ASCII
letters, digits, underscores or hyphens; start with a letter and end with a
letter or digit. Reserved names and the gh- prefix cannot be registered.
One GitHub account binds one name per server. An occupied name can be used
only by its registered owner.
.PP
Credentials are isolated by HTTPS origin. Expired or revoked credentials restart
authorization in named foreground sessions. Bans, network failures and server
errors preserve the credential and do not restart login. Background sessions
report that authorization is required; authorize in a terminal, then restart
the saved session. The legacy login subcommand remains available for older scripts.
.TP
.B ssmcp whoami
Verify and display the current GitHub identity and server. \-\-json includes
credential expiry but never the bearer token.
.TP
.B ssmcp logout
Revoke the selected client login on the server and remove its local credential.
.TP
.B ssmcp list
List online sessions the current GitHub account can control. The alias is ls;
\-\-json provides machine-readable results. Use list \-i or standalone
ssmcp \-i to select a session and enter its terminal. \-\-client ID selects
a known session; \-\-terminal ID selects an existing terminal (default main).
Ctrl-C is delivered to that terminal; Ctrl-] detaches and leaves it running.
These account commands are separate from saved local sessions list and attach.
.TP
.B ssmcp invite
Print a single-use command to copy from the logged-in device A to device B.
The invitation can be redeemed for 600 seconds by default (\-\-ttl accepts
30 through 3600). The resulting controlled session lasts at most 3600 seconds
by default (\-\-session-ttl accepts 60 through 86400).
.PP
The default command downloads a temporary client from the same server, runs
join, and removes the temporary files when it exits. B needs an outbound HTTPS
connection, plus sh and curl or wget on Linux/macOS, or PowerShell on Windows.
No SSH access or prior ssmcp installation is required. The server must publish
the matching binary using client_download_dir. Use \-\-shell sh or
\-\-shell powershell for B's terminal; the default matches A's platform.
Use \-\-no-bootstrap when B already has a compatible ssmcp installation.
Treat the generated command as a temporary credential.
.TP
.B ssmcp join
Redeem \-\-token against \-\-server and run this device as a controlled
foreground session owned by the inviting account. Temporary device credentials
remain in memory; they cannot list or control other sessions or create invites.
Keep B's terminal open. Default watchdog reconnects with the redeemed device
credential; it does not redeem the invitation again. Authorization expiry or
revocation stops retries. Ctrl-C on B stops its session and owned jobs.
Temporary join sessions cannot be saved with \-\-daemon or grant
\-\-session-control.
.PP
Account commands accept \-\-server to select an HTTPS origin. Login defaults
to https://ssmcp.lmm.best when no server has been selected. See
.UR https://github.com/LIghtJUNction/ssmcp/blob/main/docs/client-accounts.md
client account and temporary access documentation
.UE
for credentials, server routing and bootstrap requirements.
.SH PERSISTENT CLIENT SERVICE
The client service is ssmcp.service; it is separate from the remote ssmcpd gateway.
Install a system unit with an explicit non-root owner, then enable and start it:
.PP
.nf
sudo /path/to/ssmcp service install --system --user alice
sudo systemctl enable --now ssmcp
ssmcp sessions create dev --ssh --allow alice@github my-ssmcp
ssmcp attach
ssmcp attach dev
.fi
.PP
Without a name, attach is exactly attach list. Attach never creates a missing
session. Ctrl-] detaches without stopping its worker. Ctrl-C is forwarded to
the selected terminal. Use attach --read-only NAME for another observer;
only one interactive frontend owns input and terminal sizing.
.PP
sessions restart NAME confirms the old owned execution tree stopped before
starting a fresh generation. sessions close NAME (alias stop) persists stopped
intent and retains configuration. sessions start NAME explicitly restores it.
sessions remove NAME stops and deletes the saved definition.
.PP
service install defaults to the current user's persistent manager. Linux
system scope requires --system --user USER; macOS provides launchd agent and
daemon scopes; Windows provides an interactive user task and a native SCM
service entrypoint with an explicit non-LocalSystem account.
Use service install --dry-run --json to review definitions. Options follow the
service operation. --user ACCOUNT is accepted only with install --system;
the default per-user scope uses no --user option. service status, start, stop,
restart and uninstall accept --system for system scope. Windows named-account
installation reads its password through --password-stdin.
.PP
A connection started with --session-control exposes ssmcp.session.list, status,
create, restart and close through code_mode. Remote-created sessions inherit
that connection's SSH policy and remain inside its workspace; children do not
inherit management authority. This grant can manage the same user's saved
service sessions. Use a separate management connection to restart or close
the current control session.
.PP
Saved sessions pin their executable snapshot. Restart preserves that version;
remove and recreate to upgrade a saved worker. Explicit service stop preserves
running intent for restoration; closed sessions stay stopped.
.SH FIRST SSH CONNECTION
An interactive first SSH connection failure opens the GitHub authorization page on
the target ssmcp portal. Compare the browser pairing code with the terminal,
sign in, and explicitly authorize the requesting computer. The account is the
ssmcp account associated with that GitHub identity; registering a key does not
modify the SSH keys settings on github.com.
.PP
The terminal asks before installing missing OpenSSH tools, creating a dedicated
Ed25519 key, or registering an existing public key. Enter yes or y to consent.
The private key stays on this computer. A generated dedicated key has no
passphrase for unattended reconnects, as explained before creation.
.PP
After public key registration, choose a computer display name or press Return
to accept the shown default. The client preserves unrelated SSH configuration,
backs up changed content, writes an explicit managed Host alias, and checks
its effective configuration with ssh -G. It then verifies the registration
handshake and continues running. OpenSSH still performs host-key confirmation.
.PP
Use ssmcp setup to start this flow directly. Without a destination, setup uses
ssmcp.lmm.best. Use the printed SSH alias for subsequent connections. The
automatic flow requires interactive stdin and stderr, runs only once before
the current process has ever registered, and never runs inside a saved
background service. A watchdog reconnect after successful registration does
not reopen account authorization.
.PP
The client and ssmcpd portal must both support the setup endpoints. Public
binary downloads use bounded waits and verified mirror fallback; credentials,
pairing secrets and official verification metadata are never sent to public
mirrors. Package installation transactions are separate from timed downloads.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name"
Set the client display name. The default is generated from the host and process. Names are limited to 128 UTF-8 bytes and may not contain control
characters or consist only of whitespace. Duplicate names are allowed; use the
server-generated client ID to select an unambiguous target.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " title"
Set the session task shown in client discovery, code_mode's clients snapshot
and connection output. Accepts Unicode and spaces, with at most 512 UTF-8 bytes.
Empty, whitespace-only and control-character titles are rejected.
The title is independent of the display name and client ID; it does not execute
commands or change access policy. When omitted, no title field is sent.
A nonempty title requires a server that supports this metadata field.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " https://origin"
Select the HTTPS server for account operations and connections. Account commands
use the last authorized server by default. A username@server destination and
this option must select the same origin. SSH destinations cannot be combined
with this option.
.TP
.BI \-\-username " name"
Choose a server-local account name when using \-\-server. Equivalent to the
username part of username@server. Saved background sessions keep this public
name and origin, never the bearer token.
.TP
.B \-\-no-browser
Print the GitHub authorization URL and confirmation code without launching a
browser. Open the link on another device and approve the matching request.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh
Explicitly select the legacy SSH transport for a Host alias or user@host.
The \-F, \-\-identity, valued \-i and \-p options also select SSH.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Use a separate OpenSSH client configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " username@github[,username@github...]"
Allow verified GitHub users to access this connection. Repeat this option or
separate entries with commas. At most 32 distinct users are accepted; case is
normalized and duplicates are removed. The identity syntax is username@provider;
github is the currently supported provider. Usernames contain 1 to 39 ASCII
letters or digits with optional single hyphens between them.
.PP
Sharing with additional users requires an SSH destination. An HTTPS account
connection is owned by its signed-in account. This option cannot accompany
\-\-list-tools. The flag selects access policy. Existing SSH keys and
configuration remain usable; interactive first-connection setup may open the portal in a browser.
The server must support and configure GitHub OAuth. An unsupported
server causes startup to fail before exposing the local backend.
.TP
.BI \-\-portal " https://origin"
Use this HTTPS portal origin for setup when it differs from the SSH hostname.
Paths, query strings, fragments and embedded credentials are rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-no-setup
Disable automatic first-connection setup. Noninteractive commands and saved
background services do not start interactive setup even without this flag.
Cannot accompany the setup subcommand.
.TP
.BI \-\-identity " file"
Use the specified SSH private key. The historical \-i FILE HOST form remains
supported; ssh-agent is also supported. A standalone \-i without a file,
or \-\-interactive, selects remote account terminal control instead.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Connect to the specified SSH port.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " path"
Run the specified absolute shell path in a local pseudo-terminal. The default
is SHELL or /bin/sh on Unix and COMSPEC or cmd.exe on Windows.
.TP
.B \-\-list-tools
Start the local shell and discover installed harnesses, print the combined
native JSON catalog and shut them down. Does not connect to SSH and does not accept user@host. This is
the native capability catalog, not the two-tool external MCP catalog.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Save a private session definition and an executable copy. When the persistent
session manager is running, create a service-managed session; the installed
manager's startup policy restores sessions that are still expected to run.
Otherwise use the legacy per-session native manager, without configuring
boot/login startup. New sessions use watchdog by default; \-\-no-watchdog
disables it. Existing saved sessions retain their recorded watchdog policy.
A successful launch request is not proof of an SSH or Broker connection.
Cannot accompany \-\-list-tools. Existing saved names are not overwritten.
.TP
.B \-\-session-control
Expose saved-session management tools on this connection for the current OS
user. Child sessions do not inherit this authority. Use another management
connection or the local CLI to restart or close this control session.
.TP
.B \-\-no-watchdog
Disable automatic retries after connection failure or disconnection. New
foreground and background sessions enable watchdog by default. An explicit stop
takes precedence; expired or revoked account authorization also stops retries.
Disabling watchdog does not change saved sessions created earlier.
.TP
.B \-\-watchdog
Explicitly enable the default recovery policy for compatibility with existing
commands. Cannot accompany \-\-no-watchdog or \-\-list-tools.
Service-managed sessions use supervisor recovery after process failure;
legacy background sessions configure their native manager's recovery policy.
Local \-\-list-tools discovery remains a one-shot operation.
.TP
.BI \-\-reconnect-delay " seconds"
Set the delay between connection attempts to an integer from 1 through 3600.
Defaults to 5 seconds; no explicit \-\-watchdog flag is needed. Cannot
accompany \-\-no-watchdog. Legacy Windows scheduled-task failure recovery
has a separate minimum interval of 60 seconds;
service-managed workers use the configured delay.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH MCP TOOLS
.TP
.B list
With no arguments, return online client IDs, names, backend types, hostnames and
startup directories that the current authorization may access. With client_id, return
that client's native tool names, descriptions and schemas. Pass a returned
nextCursor as cursor to read another capability page.
.TP
.B code_mode
Accept a code string and optional timeout_ms. JavaScript has clients (a metadata
snapshot), await call(clientId, toolName, arguments), await tools(clientId,
cursor), print, console.log and top-level return. Calls return complete native
MCP results, including isError and structuredContent. Inspect isError even when
the JavaScript promise resolves. The code-mode structuredContent has result
(the script return value) and logs (an array of strings).
.PP
The server resolves an installed Node.js binary and validates --jitless support
before starting it inside bubblewrap for each request. Runtime manager shims are
not passed to Node.js as if they were the runtime, and discovery does not install
missing runtimes or dependencies. Node.js 18 or newer, bubblewrap 0.8 or newer and util-linux prlimit are
server dependencies managed by the operating system package manager; no npm package is needed. A client-only
ssmcp installation does not require these server dependencies. The public
JavaScript API includes clients, call, tools, logging, exec, reload, outputs.read/stat/cleanup,
fs, context, skills, agents, rsync/sync, jobs, ports, resources, browser, computer
and omarchy. Short native
results are returned unchanged. Long backend output is replaced by artifact
metadata; inspect spilled and use outputs.read. PTY artifacts contain raw terminal
bytes; command streams have separate artifacts; oversized MCP results retain
the saved MCP JSON. OpenCode HTTP responses may expose body_artifact and
body_format instead of a generic MCP-result artifact.
Native backend calls execute
on the selected client with that local user's permissions. Runtime state is
fresh for each request; backend sessions remain alive.
.PP
The bridge script is installed at PREFIX/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js, normally
/usr/local/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js. The server environment variable
SSMCP_CODE_MODE_RUNNER may select an absolute script path. Missing dependencies
or unavailable namespace isolation cause code_mode to fail; list remains usable.
.PP
Timeout stops orchestration, but an already-started native action may continue.
Inspect native state before retrying a mutating action. Retain output cursors
and agent session IDs in the calling application for subsequent requests.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B shell
Always available: an interactive local shell and PTY with terminal.read,
terminal.write, terminal.resize and terminal.status. Independent commands use
ssmcp.exec.start/list/status/read/wait/cancel/forget/events.
.TP
.B codex
Codex App Server over stdio. Requires schema generation support. The installed
CLI's generated client methods, events and approval responses are callable.
Configured MCP tools are dynamically enumerated with mcpServerStatus/list and
called through mcpServer/tool/call, under discovered codex.mcp.* routes. Only a
complete successful catalog replaces the previous snapshot. Missing native
schemas remain unavailable; the bridge does not synthesize them.
.TP
.B claude
Claude Code's native claude mcp serve entry point. Tools are discovered and
prefixed with claude.; their schemas and results are preserved. Native MCP tool
calls do not imply the interactive terminal's confirmation behavior.
.TP
.B pi
A limited native JSONL RPC bridge attached to an already initialized session
through SSMCP_PI_RPC_SOCKET. The socket path must be absolute. The socket and its direct parent must be
owned by the current UID, have no group/other permissions and not be symbolic
links. The connected peer UID is checked. The installed binary and version
must also be recognized; the get_state handshake has a five-second deadline.
Discovery sends get_state but does not spawn Pi or disable user extensions.
Without a safe existing endpoint this provider remains unavailable.
The exposed operations are pi.rpc, ssmcp.pi.events, ssmcp.pi.requests and
ssmcp.pi.respond. Internal model, plugin and MCP tool schemas are not inferred
from a known control-command list.
.TP
.B opencode
Attach to an operator-managed existing local service using SSMCP_OPENCODE_URL
and OPENCODE_PASSWORD. The URL must be http://127.0.0.1:PORT/ with an explicit
port, no userinfo, other path, query or fragment. Proxies and redirects are not
used. The native Basic username is opencode and the password must be nonempty.
Discovery reads /api/info and /openapi.json, checks native major version 2 and
a positive PID, and does not start a service, create sessions or submit prompts.
The /api/event stream is connected lazily on an explicit operation or events call. OpenCode v2.3.0 service
startup can initialize schedules and install configured plugins, so automatic
spawn is unavailable. HTTP, SSE and WebSocket operations require successful
native discovery and retain native permission decisions.
.SH MANAGED COMMANDS
Use exec.run(clientId,{command:[program,arg],wait_ms:5000}) for an independent
command. Results carry job_id, running, exit_code, stdout, stderr and byte
cursors. A successful start acknowledges launch, not completion. The working
directory must remain within the authorized workspace. Preserve pid_role when
reporting a PID; a supervisor PID is distinct from the command PID. Arguments are passed directly; shell syntax requires an explicit shell.
Use exec.parallel(clientId,commands,{concurrency:4}) to orchestrate commands in
parallel. Results preserve input order and report fulfilled/rejected per item;
a fulfilled operation may still have a nonzero process exit code.
.PP
exec.start returns immediately. exec.status, exec.wait and exec.read observe the
same job ID across code_mode requests. wait_ms is 0..60000 and ends only the
wait. timeout_ms is 0..900000 and stops the process tree when nonzero; zero is
unlimited. exec.cancel stops a running job; exec.forget removes a finished record.
exec.list shows retained jobs. Concurrency limits simultaneous start/wait/read
orchestrations; jobs still running after a wait can overlap later commands.
.PP
At most 32 commands run simultaneously. Capacity is checked before spawning.
Up to 128 records are retained, evicting the oldest completed record when full.
Each stream has a 64 KiB memory tail and an independent cursor. Read at most
32 KiB per stream; truncated flags report missed tail bytes. Long output also
has a persistent artifact: use outputs.read for its captured prefix and inspect
capture completeness. Exiting or reloading the client stops its managed work.

.SH AUTHENTICATION
Account connections use a server-issued credential over HTTPS/WebSocket.
SSH connections require public-key authentication, with standard OpenSSH
host-key verification. For GitHub-authorized connections, add the returned
/mcp URL to an OAuth-capable MCP client. Sign in with GitHub in the browser, then review the
verified identity, MCP client name, registered callback origin and resource URL
on ssmcp's separate consent page. Explicitly allow or cancel that MCP client's
request. GitHub sign-in alone does not grant the MCP client access.
.PP
The server resolves allowed GitHub usernames to stable user IDs when each
connection attaches. Discovery and calls check that connection's IDs. A rename
does not change an existing connection's identity match; reconnecting resolves
the startup usernames again. Update \-\-allow after a username change. No
cross-connection username pin is persisted. An authorized MCP client may see
an empty list until a matching connection comes online.
.PP
For SSH without a client login or \-\-allow, add the returned /username URL
and enter login_code on the OAuth page. This grant covers only clients without an allow list under that SSH
account and depends on its issuing connection. It cannot access any client
started with \-\-allow, even under the same SSH account.
.PP
Both MCP authorization modes complete Authorization Code with S256 PKCE and
use ssmcp-issued access tokens as Bearer credentials. GitHub tokens are used to read the verified
identity and are not retained. Repository and email scopes are not requested.
.PP
With \-\-ssh or explicit SSH options, the destination may be a Host alias
from ~/.ssh/config. OpenSSH resolves HostName, User, Port, IdentityFile,
IdentityAgent and ProxyJump. An explicit SSH user@host overrides the configured
User; without SSH options, username@server selects HTTPS. Use \-F for a
separate SSH configuration. Local ssh-agent authentication works without agent forwarding;
ssmcp disables forwarding, connection multiplexing and remote TTY allocation.
.PP
Public keys are managed by the server's OpenSSH authorized_keys policy. A key
already accepted for that account can be reused. On a ForceCommand-restricted
ssmcp account, update authorized_keys through a separate administrator account
or server console; ssh-copy-id and SFTP cannot execute their normal operations
through the fixed bridge command. Append the new public key, test a second
connection, then remove only the old key. Removing a key affects new SSH
authentication; close an existing connection to revoke its OAuth grants.
.PP
The login code expires 1800 seconds after issue and is consumed by one successful
authorization. login_expires_in subtracts backend startup time. MCP access
tokens default to at most 8 hours. Refresh tokens rotate on successful use and
have a default 30-day idle period that slides forward on each refresh, bounded
by a 90-day authorization-family lifetime measured from initial authorization.
Client registrations and secrets default to 365 days. Server operators can
configure these four lifetimes; tokens are also bounded by remaining grant and
registration lifetimes.
.PP
Registration without grant_types provisions both authorization_code and
refresh_token. Explicitly registering only authorization_code does not issue
a refresh token. The MCP client must implement renewal and save the latest
rotated refresh token. Before expiry, or after a 401 challenge with
error="invalid_token", it requests grant_type=refresh_token with the current
refresh token, original resource URL and registered client authentication.
It saves the new token pair and retries with the new access token. Serialize
refreshes for one grant; reusing a consumed refresh token revokes the grant.
.PP
GitHub grants are bound to the verified identity, resource and OAuth client,
without an issuing SSH connection. They may access current and future clients
whose allow lists match that identity. Login-code grants are additionally bound
to their issuing SSH connection and exclude all allow-listed clients. Closing
that source revokes its access and refresh tokens. Reconnect to obtain a new
login code when a fresh login-code authorization is needed.
.PP
Reopening an MCP application can reuse its saved, valid OAuth tokens. GitHub
grants survive local client reconnects, while session IDs and backends change;
the new connection must explicitly allow the identity. Login-code grants require
their source connection to remain alive. Access and refresh tokens remain in memory, so restarting ssmcpd requires a new
login. Configured DCR persistence retains existing client registrations. Access-token expiry can
be handled by silent refresh. Expired, revoked or replayed refresh tokens produce
invalid_grant; sign in again using the selected mode. Updating source alone does
not upgrade the running daemon.
.PP
Native agent permissions, request IDs and approval responses remain explicit.
ssmcp does not automatically answer native requests. Install and configure the
selected agent on the client; the gateway does not need that agent installed.
.SH CONTEXT AND TEXT FILES
The client provides skill metadata, instruction views and UTF-8 file
primitives through code_mode independently of any Harness. Use context.roots, skills.list/read/view,
agents.list/read/resolve and fs.readText/writeText/editText.
The workspace root is the startup directory. Repeat
.B --context-root PATH
to add up to eight existing read-only context directories. Select their returned
root IDs explicitly; these grants do not extend ordinary file writes.
Skill views index name and description frontmatter. Full documents are read on
demand. Instruction views follow the selected root to a target and preserve
source paths and contents. No model is started by these operations. Skill
selection uses a root and path; discovery and reading do not establish that a
model invoked or used a skill.
.SH FILE AND TOOL AUDIT
Successful native reads emit ssmcp.file.read. Atomic writes and edits capture
the preimage while holding the write guard and emit ssmcp.file.write and
ssmcp.file.diff only after commit. Missing, known text and unknown snapshots
are distinct. Comparable text produces actual added/removed line counts;
unknown, binary, sensitive or excessive snapshots use null counts and an
explicit reason. Failed calls do not produce successful access events.
.PP
Read ssmcp.fs.events for evidence. Small complete diffs are inline; larger
diffs use diff_artifact in the shared output store. preview is display-only.
Inspect diff_complete, diff_unavailable_reason and diff_redacted. External
file effects remain unknown unless observed through a native interface.
Harness lifecycle events identify the provider, tool, call, duration and
success without inferring effects from names or arguments.
.PP
Context emits skill.discovered, skill.read and discovery summary events under
the ssmcp prefix. An invoked state requires explicit native evidence; finding
a SKILL.md does not execute it.

.SH CLIENT RELOAD
After installing a replacement at the original client entry point, use
.B await reload(clientId)
inside code_mode. The native equivalent is
.BR "call(clientId, \(dqssmcp.client.reload\(dq, {})" .
The request uses the existing client authorization and accepts no arguments.
Reload is refused while other tool requests are in flight or the entry point
is unavailable. On acceptance it returns accepted:true and status:restarting,
waits for the server acknowledgement, stops the old connection's background
commands and forwards, closes Harness bridges, and re-executes the installed
entry point. Existing external Pi/OpenCode services remain operator-managed.
.PP
Startup arguments, working directory and environment are preserved, including
title and allow policy. Reload does not download, build or install an update.
Use list again to confirm reconnection and select the new client ID by title,
name and cwd. A restarting acknowledgement is not proof of reconnection.
Valid GitHub identity grants can continue accessing the matching new connection;
login-code mode requires a fresh OAuth login. Upgrade both client and server
before using reload. Old clients without the reload tool require a manual
restart for their first upgrade.
.PP
A saved background session reloads its saved executable, not an independently
updated installation path. Remove and recreate that session to change its image.

.SH BACKGROUND SESSIONS
Run sessions commands on the client machine. They manage saved configuration
names, not Broker client IDs, and do not add public MCP tools. All commands accept
\-\-json. A saved name is 1 to 128 UTF-8 bytes, cannot start with a hyphen,
and cannot contain control characters, path separators or surrounding whitespace.
Quote names containing internal spaces. Saved names are unique within the chosen
state directory; ordinary foreground display names may still repeat.
.TP
.B ssmcp sessions list
List saved local sessions, including stopped sessions. Use ssmcp list for
online account sessions; sessions list \-\-remote is an account-list alias.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions status " NAME
Report manager_state, manager_active and manager_pid separately from
connection_state. The latter is connected, disconnected or unknown. An active
manager or a zero CLI exit does not prove Broker readiness. The phase and attempt
fields record observed lifecycle progress; a new connection gets a new client ID.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions start " NAME
Start a stopped session using its saved executable and configuration.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions stop " NAME
Request stop, suppress automatic recovery and confirm native termination.
The watchdog does not revive an explicitly stopped session.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions restart " NAME
Stop and start the same saved configuration. Does not install an updated image.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions logs " "NAME [--lines N]"
Read bounded logs; N defaults to 50 and accepts 1 through 1000. JSON identifies
source, fallback, warning, coverage, events, limit and truncated; legacy native
sessions also report their unit. Service-managed sessions expose lifecycle_only
coverage on every platform; use attach for live tool and terminal output.
Legacy Linux sessions read the owned unit's journal, falling back explicitly to
lifecycle records when that journal is unavailable or empty. Journal events
contain timestamp_us, pid and an audited event parsed from MESSAGE, without
journal environment or command metadata. Legacy macOS and Windows sessions
expose lifecycle_only coverage. Sources are not concatenated; lifecycle records
are not proof of complete tool output.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions auth " NAME
Explicitly read a current login code from the private auth record. Ordinary
status and lifecycle logs do not contain it. Normal disconnect handling clears
the record; auth refuses a session without a current connected observation.
GitHub-authorized connections do not issue login codes.
.TP
.BI "ssmcp sessions remove " NAME
Stop the session and remove only its saved definition, executable and lifecycle
records. Connection output files retain their separate retention policy.
.PP
sessions create requires the persistent session manager. --daemon also uses that
manager when it is running; previously saved legacy native sessions keep their
original management mechanism. Service-managed workers follow the installed
manager's startup scope. Stopping that manager preserves running intent, while
closing an individual session keeps it stopped after the manager restarts.
.PP
The following native policies apply only to legacy per-session registrations.
Linux uses an existing user systemd manager and a transient service; macOS uses a
current-user launchd agent; Windows uses a current logged-in user's scheduled
task, without administrator or SYSTEM credentials. These legacy registrations
create no boot/login trigger or Linux linger setting. Continued operation after
logout depends on the existing user manager and login session.
.PP
For those legacy registrations, watchdog configures Linux Restart=on-failure
with the selected RestartSec, or macOS unsuccessful-exit recovery with the
selected ThrottleInterval. Legacy Windows task recovery uses
max(60, reconnect-delay) seconds and up to 255 restarts. This native limit does
not apply to service-managed workers or SSH retries inside a live client.
Manual stop takes precedence over every recovery policy. New saved sessions
enable watchdog by default. Older saved manifests keep their recorded policy,
including disabled watchdog; \-\-no-watchdog is stored explicitly in new
disabled definitions.
.PP
Default state roots are $XDG_STATE_HOME/ssmcp/sessions, or
~/.local/state/ssmcp/sessions on Linux; ~/Library/Application Support/ssmcp/sessions
on macOS; and %LOCALAPPDATA%\essmcp\esessions on Windows.
SSMCP_SESSION_HOME may select a private absolute path; use the same value when
creating and managing sessions. Unix directories and records are private to
their owner. The saved image is checked against its SHA-256.
.PP
The definition records cwd, foreground arguments and PATH/SSH_AUTH_SOCK references;
it does not copy the launching shell's entire environment. HOME comes from the
user manager. Keep the workspace, SSH files and tool paths available.
To upgrade, preserve the arguments and needed results, remove the saved session,
then recreate it with the newly installed binary from its intended workspace.
Disconnect still cleans old jobs, PTY, forwarding and tool routes; watchdog does
not provide persistent cross-connection commands or output authorization.

.SH LONG OUTPUT
Output exceeding 32 KiB or 500 lines is stored in a private client directory.
Linux uses /tmp/ssmcp/<uid>; other platforms use a user-specific temporary
directory. Use the returned artifact ID and path rather than assuming a path.
The result carries metadata instead of the full output: ID, path, SHA-256,
byte and line counts, observed_bytes, complete and expires_at_unix.
The digest covers the first N bytes at that snapshot, where N is the bytes field.
Lines count newline
records and a final nonempty unterminated record. Complete describes capture
integrity, not whether the command succeeded.
.PP
A PTY artifact
covers the whole terminal session's merged raw stream, including terminal control
bytes. Independent command artifacts preserve stdout and stderr separately. Oversized
MCP results are stored as their original MCP result JSON. OpenCode HTTP bodies
retain their body_format and separate body_artifact, and short JSON bodies
remain structured objects. Output already truncated by a native harness cannot be recovered.
.PP
Use outputs.read(clientId,{id,offset,max_bytes}) for a chunk, with byte offsets,
16 KiB by default and 32 KiB maximum. Reuse next_offset. The result contains text
and exact data_base64; its SHA-256 covers only the returned chunk. EOF refers to
the current size and does not mean an active capture has finished.
Use outputs.stat(clientId,{id}) for current metadata and a whole-file snapshot
digest. The native tools are ssmcp.output.read and ssmcp.output.stat.
.PP
Retention is 24 hours from the latest write or completion. Completed artifacts
are limited to 512 MiB in total, with oldest files evicted first. Each artifact
is limited to 256 MiB; exceeding it or failing to write marks an existing saved
prefix incomplete and supplies an error. If artifact creation itself fails,
the operation reports an error and may have no readable artifact.
Active captures hold file locks and are skipped
by cleanup, so their bytes may temporarily exceed the completed-file quota.
Unrelated files are preserved. Output access is scoped to the owning
connection; a new client ID cannot read another connection's artifacts,
background tasks or audit records merely because it shares the OS account.
.PP
Cleanup runs at output-store startup, artifact creation and every 60 seconds
while the client runs. outputs.cleanup(clientId), or the native
ssmcp.output.cleanup tool with empty arguments, runs the same policy immediately
and returns removed and retained counts. It does not unconditionally empty the
directory. Cleanup resumes at next startup if all clients have exited.
The local --max-inline-output display setting does not change these agent-facing
output thresholds.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ssmcp alice@ssmcp.example.com --name laptop
ssmcp alice@ssmcp.example.com --no-browser
ssmcp whoami
ssmcp --name laptop
ssmcp list
ssmcp -i --client CLIENT_ID
ssmcp invite --shell sh
ssmcp invite --shell powershell
ssmcp --no-watchdog --name one-attempt
ssmcp --ssh --name build-shell user@host
ssmcp --ssh --name build-shell my-server
ssmcp --ssh --name build-shell --title "Fix the client build" my-server
ssmcp --ssh --allow octocat@github --name build-shell my-server
ssmcp --ssh --allow alice@github,bob@github --allow carol@github my-server
ssmcp --ssh --name review --title "Review project" user@host
ssmcp --list-tools
.fi
.PP
Example JavaScript supplied as code_mode's code argument, using an ID from list:
.nf
return await call("CLIENT_ID_FROM_LIST", "terminal.status", {});
.fi
